goer@quads.uchicago.edu (Richard L. Goerwitz) writes:
>
>Okay, you are a shopkeeper in a seedy, dark dungeon.  You see a tourist
>come in with a lot of money, a camera, and gaudy clothing.  What do you
>do?  You take the sucker for everything he's worth.
>
>This scenario underscores a big conceptual problem I have with one, small
>aspect of NetHack.  On the matter of charisma, it seeme to run *exactly
>opposite* to the way charisma works in the real world.  In the real world,
>the tourist would be about as un-charismatic as one could possibly ima-
>gine, and could not expect to bargain well with people such as one might
>meet in a great dungeon.
>
>I'd suggest giving the tourist the worst charisma numbers of any charac-
>ter.  I know that the charisma thing is one of the big attractions of
>the tourist character, but it's so directly opposite what we experience
>in our daily lives that some modification is, in my opinion, needed.

Oh, I think you need to check out the beach bimbettes at Makaha
or Lauterdale, or the Surfer Dudes at Virginia Beach.  It's only
us middle aged tubby aloha shirt wearing gawkers that are
non-charismatic.  There's plenty of body beautifuls among the jet set.

>Oh, BTW:  Ditto on rings of adornment.  Male characters should not be
>able to have their charisma increased by rings of adornment.  Men with
>gaudy jewelry aren't suddenly going to be treated better by avaricious
>shopkeepers.  The sparkle of the gem, moreover, might attract certain
>kinds of monsters, for better or worse.

Well, aside from the umbrage I take at somebody that doesn't like my
citrine or topaz rings, the silly thing is *magic*, not intrinsically
beautiful.  It's like the one George Burns wears (woven out of $1000
bills) to keep all those decolletage wearers flocking around him.
A green paper ring may not be pretty, but it does make _him_ attractive.

;-)

